/-!
# Minlos' theorem

A function `φ` of positive type on a real topological vector space `E`, with `φ 0 = 1`, is the
Fourier transform `x ↦ ∫ e^{i ω x} dμ(ω)` of a Radon probability measure `μ` on the weak dual
as soon as, for every `ε > 0`,

  `1 - Re φ ≤ ε + ∑ₙ ωₙ²`,   `ωₙ² ≤ cₙ² H`,   `∑ₙ cₙ² < ∞`,

for a continuous positive quadratic form `H` and linear forms `ωₙ` [Bourbaki, *INT*, IX, §6,
no. 10, Thm 2][bourbaki2004integration2]. This is Bourbaki's continuity of `φ` for the Sazonov
topology, with the relative trace `Tr (∑ₙ ωₙ² / H) ≤ ∑ₙ cₙ²` written out, so the statement needs
nothing beyond mathlib's vocabulary; on a Hilbert space it is Sazonov's condition
(`MeasureTheory.exists_isProbabilityMeasure_charFun_eq_iff_sazonov`). `φ` is not assumed
continuous, and `E` is neither assumed locally convex, nor separable, nor metrizable. The measure
is unique among Radon measures (`MeasureTheory.ext_of_integral_cexp_eq`).

The proof is Bourbaki's. Bochner's theorem gives the marginals on `ℝ^J`, `J ⊆ E` finite,
consistent by uniqueness of the Fourier transform. They are uniformly concentrated on the images
of the weak-* ellipsoids `{ω | ω² ≤ r² H}`: these are compact (Alaoglu–Bourbaki), their images in
`ℝ^J` are the finite-dimensional ellipsoids (Hahn–Banach), and outside those Minlos's lemma leaves
mass at most `3 (ε + ∑ₙ cₙ² / r²)`. Prokhorov's theorem on projective limits
(`MeasureTheory.IsProjectiveMeasureFamily.exists_map_restrict_eq`) then gives the measure on the
Borel σ-algebra of the weak dual — not only on the σ-algebra generated by the evaluations, the
σ-algebra of a Kolmogorov extension, which is in general strictly smaller. Gel'fand and Vilenkin
prove the theorem for countably Hilbert nuclear spaces [Gel'fand–Vilenkin, IV, Ch. IV, §2.3,
Thm 3][gelfand1964generalized4], and [Douglas–Hoback][douglas2026bochner] formalize it through the
evaluation σ-algebra of a separable one.

On a nuclear locally convex space every continuous `φ` satisfies the condition
(`NuclearSpace.exists_sq_le_tsum_sq`), and on a barrelled space every Fourier transform of a Radon
measure is continuous; so on a barrelled nuclear space — `𝓢(E, ℝ)`, whose weak dual is the space
of tempered distributions — the Fourier transform is a bijection from the Radon probability
measures on the weak dual onto the continuous positive-definite functions with `φ 0 = 1`
[Bourbaki, *INT*, IX, §6, no. 12, Cor.][bourbaki2004integration2], and from the finite ones onto
the continuous positive-definite functions. A continuous positive quadratic form `Q` is thus the
covariance of a Gaussian measure on the dual, `exp (-Q / 2)` being positive-definite: the
Euclidean free field when `Q f = ⟪f, (m² - Δ)⁻¹ f⟫` on `𝓢(ℝᵈ, ℝ)`.

## Main results

* `MeasureTheory.measure_setOf_exists_lt_inner_sq_le`: Minlos's lemma
* `MeasureTheory.ext_of_integral_cexp_eq`: a Radon measure on the weak dual is determined by its
  Fourier transform
* `MeasureTheory.continuous_integral_cexp`: on a barrelled space, it is continuous
* `MeasureTheory.exists_isProbabilityMeasure_integral_cexp_eq`: Minlos' theorem
* `MeasureTheory.exists_isProbabilityMeasure_integral_cexp_eq_of_continuous`: on a nuclear space
* `MeasureTheory.exists_isProbabilityMeasure_integral_cexp_eq_iff`,
  `MeasureTheory.exists_integral_cexp_eq_iff`: the Bochner–Minlos theorem on a barrelled nuclear
  space, for probability and for finite measures
* `posSemidef_circulant_exp_neg_half`: `exp (-Q / 2)` is positive-definite
* `MeasureTheory.exists_isProbabilityMeasure_map_eq_gaussianReal`: Gaussian measures on the dual of
  a nuclear space

